## Beat 1 — Bare-term global search (0:30–2:30)

**[Screen: run `summarize_subjects('melanoma')`]**

> This is the whole query. One word. I'm not telling it which column to look in — melanoma could be a diagnosis, an anatomic site, a note on a file. CDA just searches everywhere at once.

**[Screen: result renders]**

> `[PLACEHOLDER: X,XXX subjects]` — from one word, zero setup.

> And if I add a couple of columns so I can actually see what matched —

**[Screen: run `summarize_subjects('melanoma', add_columns=['anatomic_site', 'diagnosis', 'format'])`]**

> — now I can see *why*. Here's something fun: watch the anatomic_site breakdown. I typed "melanoma," which is mostly a skin cancer, so you'd expect "skin" to dominate. But —

**[Screen: highlight a surprising non-skin value in results, e.g. `[PLACEHOLDER: eye / uveal, or lymph node]`]**

> `[PLACEHOLDER: name the surprising value]` shows up too, with real numbers behind it. That's not a mistake. When CDA finds a subject through melanoma, it shows you *everything* about that subject — including data that has nothing to do with why they matched in the first place. You didn't ask for it, but now you know it's there.

> There's a second kind of surprise hiding in here too — more specific terms than the one I typed.

**[Screen: highlight more specific anatomic_site terms under "skin," e.g. `[PLACEHOLDER: "skin of trunk," "skin of limb"]`]**

> CDA's anatomic_site field is backed by a real medical ontology, so a broad term automatically pulls in everything more specific underneath it in that tree. I never had to know that hierarchy existed for this to work.

---

## Beat 2 — THE NEW THING: real controlled-vocabulary terms (2:30–4:30)

**[Screen: same query, now adding `add_extras=['all']`]**

> Okay, here's what's actually new. Up until recently, CDA could tell you *that* a result matched through this kind of rollup — but not *which* term did the matching. Now it can.

**[Screen: run `summarize_subjects('melanoma', add_columns=['anatomic_site', 'diagnosis', 'format'], add_extras=['all'])`]**

> Same subjects. Same count. `add_extras` doesn't change what matched — it just finally tells you *why*.

**[Screen: result renders, scroll to the diagnosis table with its new columns]**

> Same subjects. Same count. `add_extras` doesn't change what matched — it just finally tells you *why*. And this table is the best proof of it: look at every melanoma subtype here — nodular, spindle cell, epithelioid, amelanotic, even "malignant melanoma" itself. They all share the exact same label: "Nevi and melanomas." One shared category, explaining why a dozen different-sounding diagnoses all count together. That's CDA's actual classification logic, spelled out, for the first time.

**[Screen: scroll up to anatomic_site, highlight `uvea`'s slim_term showing "eye"]**

> Same idea on the anatomy side — `uvea` now explicitly lists "eye" as its category. That confirms what we guessed from the matching counts a second ago. It's not a coincidence, it's the actual ontology, visible now instead of hidden.

> Quick honesty check: this only does something interesting on two fields right now — anatomic_site and disease. Those are the two backed by a real external ontology. Other fields, like sex or species, are fully standardized but flat — there's no "more specific" version of female. And a few fields aren't harmonized yet at all. That's not a bug, that's just where the harmonization work is right now.

